### Set PORT in backend/.env to match (main.ts falls back to 3000).
### NOTE: the app has no setGlobalPrefix, and controllers are @Controller('auth'),
### @Controller('posts'), ... — so paths are /auth/..., NOT /api/auth/...
@host = http://localhost:4000

### =====================================================================
### Auth & Membership
### =====================================================================

### A1. Request OTP
POST {{host}}/auth/register/request-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test.new@ku.th",
  "password": "test1234"
}

### A2. Verify OTP (เอา OTP จาก terminal backend log มาแทน)
POST {{host}}/auth/register/verify-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test.new@ku.th",
  "otp": "000000"
}

### A3. Login (ใช้ email/password ที่สมัครผ่าน OTP flow ข้างบนแล้วเท่านั้น)
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test.new@ku.th",
  "password": "test1234"
}

### A4. Login ผิดรหัสผ่าน -> expect 401
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test.new@ku.th",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}

### =====================================================================
### §7.7 Report & Moderation
### =====================================================================
###
### Runs against the mock data seeded by init.sql (docker compose up).
### Users:  1 = admin (isAdmin), 2-12 = regular users
### Seeded reports (only #1 and #2 are used below; #3-#5 are already decided
### or unrelated, so they are left alone):
###   #1  Transaction / target_id 1 / reporter 3 / Under_Investigation / handled_by 1
###       └─ Transaction 1: buyer 3, seller 2  → reported party = seller 2
###       └─ already has Appeal #1 (by user 2, Pending)
###   #2  Post / target_id 5 / reporter 6 / Pending
###       └─ Post 5 → Item 5 → owner 5        → reported party = user 5
###   #3  User / target_id 11 / Violation_Confirmed  → already has Appeal #2 (Rejected)
###   #4  Post / target_id 1  / Report_Dismissed
###   #5  User / target_id 9  / Pending
###
### The reported party is resolved by joining the real tables (see
### AppealService.resolveReportedUserId), so these ids come from the DB.
###
### Request bodies use snake_case (those are the DTO field names). Responses
### come straight from Prisma, whose model fields are camelCase — hence
### `$.reportId` / `$.appealId` in the chained references below.

### 1. POST /reports — no auth header -> expect 401
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "report_type": "User",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam"
}

### 2. POST /reports — with x-user-id -> expect 201 + status: Pending
# @name createReport
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2

{
  "report_type": "User",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam",
  "details": "Sending unsolicited messages in chat",
  "evidence_urls": ["https://example.com/evidence1.jpg"]
}

### 3. POST /reports — invalid report_type -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2

{
  "report_type": "NotARealType",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam"
}

### 4. GET /admin/reports — no admin header -> expect 403
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 2

### 5. GET /admin/reports — as admin -> expect 200 + list
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

### 6. POST /admin/reports/:id/resolve — resolve the report from #2 -> expect 200
### run #2 first so the id resolves
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}}/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed",
  "resolution_note": "Confirmed after reviewing chat history."
}

### 7. POST /admin/reports/:id/resolve — resolve the SAME report again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}}/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Report_Dismissed"
}

### 8. POST /admin/reports/999/resolve — nonexistent report -> expect 404
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/999/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed"
}

### === Appeals — reported party resolved by real DB join ===

### 9. POST /appeals — wrong user appeals the report from #2 -> expect 403
### #2 is report_type User with target_id 4, so only user 4 may appeal it.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 7

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Not my report"
}

### 10. POST /appeals — the reported user (4) appeals -> expect 201, status Pending
# @name createAppeal
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Those messages were about a legitimate transaction.",
  "evidence_urls": ["https://example.com/appeal-evidence.jpg"]
}

### 11. POST /appeals — appeal the same report twice -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Trying again"
}

### 12. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — same admin who resolved the report (1) -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Approved"
}

### 13. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — a different admin -> expect 200
### Approving also flips the report to Report_Dismissed (verify with #5).
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 9
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Approved",
  "resolution_note": "Reviewed chat log, messages were transaction-related."
}

### 14. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — decide the SAME appeal again -> expect 400
### Without this guard the appeal would flip to Rejected while its report stayed
### Report_Dismissed, and the original handledBy/handledAt would be overwritten.
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 10
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Rejected",
  "resolution_note": "Attempt to overwrite an already-approved appeal"
}

### === Reported-party resolution for Post / Transaction reports ===
### These use the seeded reports, so run them on a fresh `docker compose up`
### (report #2 must still be unresolved for the appeal window check to pass).

### 15. POST /admin/reports/2/resolve — resolve the seeded Post report -> expect 200
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/2/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed"
}

### 16. POST /appeals — wrong user appeals report 2 -> expect 403
### Report 2 targets Post 5, whose Item is owned by user 5 — user 4 is unrelated.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": 2,
  "reason": "Not my post"
}

### 17. POST /appeals — the real Post owner (user 5) appeals -> expect 201
### Resolved via Post 5 -> Item 5 -> ownerId 5.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 5

{
  "report_id": 2,
  "reason": "The description matched the actual condition."
}

### 18. POST /appeals — reporter of the Transaction report tries to appeal -> expect 403
### Report 1 targets Transaction 1 (buyer 3, seller 2) and was filed by user 3,
### so the reported party is the counterparty (seller 2), not the reporter.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 3

{
  "report_id": 1,
  "reason": "I filed this report myself"
}

### =====================================================================
### §7.4 Transactions, Fulfillment & Payment
### =====================================================================
###
### AUTH: these endpoints use the real JwtAuthGuard, so every request needs
###   Authorization: Bearer <accessToken>
### Get one by logging in (A3 above) and copying `accessToken` from the response.
###
### ⚠️ The seeded mock users CANNOT log in — their password_hash in init.sql is a
### 28-character placeholder, not a real 60-character bcrypt hash. So either:
###   a) register a fresh user via A1/A2, then seed a transaction for them, or
###   b) mint a token locally:
###      node -e "console.log(require('jsonwebtoken').sign({userId:3,email:'x@ku.th',isAdmin:false},process.env.ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET,{expiresIn:'15m'}))"
###      (run from backend/ with ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET loaded from .env)
###
### Seeded transactions (docker compose up):
###   #1 Meetup   buyer 3  seller 2  Processing  (paid)     → QR flow
###   #2 Meetup   buyer 6  seller 2  Completed
###   #3 Shipping buyer 8  seller 5  Pending                → payment + ship flow
###   #4 Shipping buyer 10 seller 6  Processing
###   #5 Meetup   buyer 4  seller 5  Cancelled              → refund flow
###   #6 Meetup   buyer 2  seller 7  Completed
###
### Paste your token here once and every request below picks it up.
@token = PASTE_ACCESS_TOKEN_HERE

### T1. GET /transactions — no token -> expect 401
GET {{host}}/transactions

### T2. GET /transactions — garbage token -> expect 401
GET {{host}}/transactions
Authorization: Bearer garbage

### T3. GET /transactions — only the caller's own transactions come back
GET {{host}}/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T4. GET /transactions/1 — a party to it -> expect 200
GET {{host}}/transactions/1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T5. GET /transactions/1 — a non-party -> expect 403
GET {{host}}/transactions/1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T6. GET /transactions/999 — nonexistent -> expect 404
GET {{host}}/transactions/999
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### --- Payment (§8.6) — run against tx 3, which starts Pending ---

### T7. POST /payment-slip — as the SELLER -> expect 403 (buyer-only action)
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/payment-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "slip_image_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/3_slip.jpg"
}

### T8. POST /payment-slip — empty body -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/payment-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{}

### T9. POST /payment-slip — as the buyer (8) -> expect 200, Pending → Processing
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/payment-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "slip_image_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/3_slip.jpg"
}

### T10. POST /payment-slip — again, no longer Pending -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/payment-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "slip_image_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/3_slip.jpg"
}

### T11. POST /confirm-payment-received — as the buyer -> expect 403 (seller-only)
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T12. POST /confirm-payment-received — as the seller (5) -> expect 200
### Status stays Processing: this is only the 1st of the 2 conditions for Completed.
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T13. POST /confirm-payment-received — again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-payment-received
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### --- Shipping delivery (§8.6) — continues on tx 3 ---

### T14. POST /ship — as the buyer -> expect 403 (seller-only)
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/ship
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "tracking_number": "TH1234567890",
  "estimated_delivery_date": "2026-08-10"
}

### T15. POST /ship — malformed date -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/ship
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "tracking_number": "TH1234567890",
  "estimated_delivery_date": "not-a-date"
}

### T16. POST /confirm-delivery — before the seller shipped -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-delivery
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T17. POST /ship — as the seller (5) -> expect 200, still Processing
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/ship
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "tracking_number": "TH1234567890",
  "estimated_delivery_date": "2026-08-10"
}

### T18. POST /ship on a Meetup transaction (tx 1) -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/ship
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "tracking_number": "TH1234567890",
  "estimated_delivery_date": "2026-08-10"
}

### T19. POST /confirm-delivery — as the buyer (8) -> expect 200
### 2nd condition met → status becomes Completed and the Item becomes Transferred (§8.2).
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-delivery
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T20. POST /confirm-delivery — again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/confirm-delivery
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### --- Meetup QR (§8.6) — tx 1 is Meetup, Processing, already paid ---
### The SELLER holds the QR on screen and the BUYER scans it, which is what
### proves the goods physically changed hands.

### T21. POST /qr/generate — as the buyer -> expect 403 (seller-only)
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/qr/generate
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T22. POST /qr/generate — on a Shipping transaction (tx 3) -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/3/qr/generate
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T23. POST /qr/generate — as the seller (2) -> expect 200
### Returns { token, url, expiresInSeconds: 900 }. The token lives in Redis under
### qr_token:1 and is single-use. Copy it into T25/T26 below.
# @name generateQr
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/qr/generate
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T24. POST /qr/scan — as the seller -> expect 403 (only the buyer may scan)
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/qr/scan
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "token": "{{generateQr.response.body.$.token}}"
}

### T25. POST /qr/scan — wrong token -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/qr/scan
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "token": "wrong-token"
}

### T26. POST /qr/scan — as the buyer (3) with the right token -> expect 200
### → Completed + Item Transferred, and the token is deleted from Redis.
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/qr/scan
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "token": "{{generateQr.response.body.$.token}}"
}

### T27. POST /qr/scan — replay the same token -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/1/qr/scan
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "token": "{{generateQr.response.body.$.token}}"
}

### --- Refund evidence (§10 issue #2) — tx 5 is Cancelled ---
### These record that the two parties settled up off-platform. They move no
### money, grant nobody the power to force a refund, and never change `status`.
### Requires the buyer to have actually paid before it was cancelled, so seed:
###   UPDATE "Transaction" SET payment_confirmed_by_buyer_at = NOW() WHERE transaction_id = 5;

### T28. POST /refund-slip — on a Processing transaction (tx 4) -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/4/refund-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "refund_slip_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/4_refund.jpg"
}

### T29. POST /refund-slip — as the buyer -> expect 403 (seller refunds)
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/refund-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "refund_slip_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/5_refund.jpg"
}

### T30. POST /confirm-refund — before any slip was uploaded -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/confirm-refund
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T31. POST /refund-slip — as the seller (5) -> expect 200, status stays Cancelled
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/refund-slip
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{
  "refund_slip_url": "https://r2.example.com/slips/5_refund.jpg"
}

### T32. POST /confirm-refund — as the seller -> expect 403 (buyer confirms)
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/confirm-refund
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T33. POST /confirm-refund — as the buyer (4) -> expect 200, status stays Cancelled
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/confirm-refund
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### T34. POST /confirm-refund — again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/transactions/5/confirm-refund
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### --- NOT IMPLEMENTED YET ---
### POST /transactions/{id}/cancel-request and .../respond are blocked pending a
### team decision: init.sql has only `cancellation_requested_by`, with no column
### for the reason, the request time, or whether it was accepted/rejected — all
### three of which §8.7 needs (a rejected request must keep the confirm buttons
### locked, and "no response within the window" needs a timestamp to measure).
